## Handling Multiple Values Together

When several patterns should produce the same result, use `|` (pipe) to combine them:

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let day = 3;

  let day_type = match day {
    1 | 7 => "Weekend",
    2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 => "Weekday",
    _ => "Invalid",
  };

  io.println(day_type);
  return 0;
}
```

### The | Operator in Match

`|` means "or" — match this pattern OR that pattern:

```xiom
match code {
  200 | 201 | 204 => "Success",
  400 | 404 => "Client Error",
  500 | 502 | 503 => "Server Error",
  _ => "Unknown",
}
```

### Organizing Arms

Group related values on the same line:

```xiom
let category = match age {
  0 | 1 | 2 => "Toddler",
  3 | 4 | 5 => "Preschool",
  6 | 7 | 8 | 9 | 10 | 11 | 12 => "Child",
  13 | 14 | 15 | 16 | 17 => "Teenager",
  _ => "Adult",
};
```

### Mixing Patterns

You can mix single values, `|` patterns, and ranges in one match:

```xiom
match score {
  100 => "Perfect!",
  90 | 91 | 92 | 93 | 94 | 95 | 96 | 97 | 98 | 99 => "Excellent",
  80..90 => "Good",
  70..80 => "Average",
  60..70 => "Passing",
  _ => "Needs work",
};
```

### When to Use | vs Ranges

```xiom
// Use | for scattered values
match char {
  'a' | 'e' | 'i' | 'o' | 'u' => "Vowel",
  _ => "Consonant",
}

// Use ranges for contiguous values
match number {
  1..10 => "Small",
  10..100 => "Medium",
  _ => "Large",
}
```

### Match is Exhaustive

XIOM checks that your match covers ALL possible values:

```xiom
// Compile error if Bool isn't fully covered
match flag {
  true => "yes",
  false => "no",  // Must have both or use _
}
```

